yeastar_api
===========

The yeastar_api module of the Tryton application platform.

Installing
----------

See INSTALL

Webhook capture
---------------

The webhook receiver stores signed events before acknowledging them. PBXs
default to Capture Only mode. Automatic activity creation is opt-in and runs
outside the HTTP request, through a scheduled action.

Deployment:

1. Deploy the module, upgrade it in the target database using the usual
   trytond-admin configuration, and restart all Tryton HTTP processes::

       trytond-admin -c <server-config> -d nantic -u yeastar_api

2. Ensure the HTTPS reverse proxy forwards POST requests for
   /<database>/yeastar/webhook/<pbx-id> to Tryton without changing the body or
   removing the X-Signature header.
3. Open Administration > Yeastar > Yeastar PBX, then the Webhook tab.
   Set Public Base URL to https://nantic.nan-tic.com and save. The calculated
   Webhook URL includes the current database name and the saved PBX ID.
   With database nantic and PBX 1 it is:
   https://nantic.nan-tic.com/nantic/yeastar/webhook/1
4. In Yeastar Integrations > API, add a POST webhook using this URL. Copy
   Yeastar's generated signing secret into Tryton's Webhook Secret field.
   Enable the webhook in Tryton and save before sending a connectivity test.
5. Subscribe to 30011 (call state), 30012 (call end/CDR), 30013 (transfer)
   and 30014 (forwarding). Enable the relevant extension/trunk call monitors.
   The defaults of 5 seconds timeout and 3 retries are suitable for capture.
6. Send Yeastar's connectivity test. A signed test returns HTTP 204 and
   appears in the PBX's Webhook Events list with event type "test".

The receiver verifies Base64-encoded HMAC-SHA256 of the raw request body.
Disabled or unknown PBXs return 404, invalid signatures return 403, and
invalid JSON/event envelopes return 400. All accepted payloads are stored
in the database before acknowledgment. Exact repeated bodies are captured
once per PBX, including concurrent retries; this is transport deduplication,
not a business rule for call durations. A copied PBX has its webhook disabled
and does not inherit the secret or captured events.

Events are read-only to Yeastar administrators and restricted by company.
The secret is accessible only to Yeastar administrators. Captures include
call metadata; the secret and signature are not stored in event payloads.
New event types and nested message formats are preserved for inspection.

Capture an external call with a blind transfer, an attended transfer,
a failed transfer, and a return to the original employee. Record the test
times and extensions, and compare the captured messages with recording/CDR
entries. Preserve distinct recording IDs, CDR UIDs and live call IDs.

To export a capture window from a fresh trytond-console session::

    import json
    from datetime import datetime
    Event = pool.get('yeastar.webhook.event')
    events = Event.search([
        ('pbx', '=', 1),
        ('create_date', '>=', datetime(2026, 9, 29, 14, 0)),
        ('create_date', '<', datetime(2026, 9, 29, 15, 0)),
        ], order=[('create_date', 'ASC'), ('id', 'ASC')])
    print(json.dumps([{
        'received_at': str(event.create_date),
        'payload': json.loads(event.payload),
        } for event in events], indent=2))

Replace the PBX ID and UTC time window with the values of the actual test.
For a copied database, disable webhook reception before publishing it and
configure a separate secret if it needs its own webhook.

Automatic contact directory
---------------------------

Upgrading the module installs Synchronize Yeastar Contacts and Phonebooks,
once a day at 04:00 in the Tryton server timezone. Configure its Companies
and adjust the schedule in Scheduled Actions. The cron processes PBXs with
at least one phonebook configured in Tryton, without requiring an employee.

Each run loads active phone/mobile contact mechanisms, updates names and
numbers, publishes contacts and reconciles phonebook membership. When
party_company is installed, the existing current-company filter applies.
Contacts are tracked separately for each PBX. Select all company contacts
includes all company contacts on Yeastar; Select specific company contacts
uses exactly the contacts selected in Tryton. Newly discovered contacts are
not automatically added to a specific-selection phonebook.

Deleted, inactive, invalid or synchronization-disabled source contacts are
removed from the PBX using their saved remote IDs. Inactive/disabled contacts
retain their local link and phonebook selections for later reactivation.
Deleted source mechanisms leave a null link until the next synchronization
deletes their remote contact and local tracking row. Unlinking a contact from
a specific phonebook changes membership only. Contacts created directly on
the PBX without a Tryton tracking record are not deleted.

Successful remote creations/deletions are saved incrementally, so a failure
later in the run preserves completed work. Errors remain visible through
the scheduled action and the next execution retries pending operations.
As with the existing manual integration, a lost response to a remote create
cannot be reconciled automatically: inspect Yeastar before retrying it.
Run the cron only from the authoritative database for that PBX: a test copy
contains the same remote IDs and would modify the live directory.

Automatic activities
--------------------

Caller identification compares normalized phone/mobile numbers of active
parties. It assigns only unique matches and preserves manually assigned
parties. With phonenumbers installed, national numbers use the country of the
company party's address; otherwise only explicit international numbers can
be matched. Prefixes + and 00 and ordinary formatting are normalized. No
suffix guessing is performed. The nantic module adds a pending-caller
resolution workflow on top of this matching.

CDR imports use /openapi/v2.0/cdr/search, independently of the PBX API path
used by other services. The CDR cron requires a company. It reads all pages
in ascending time order until an empty page and revisits the previous day
to update late results by PBX and UID instead of creating duplicates.
Legacy duplicate rows are not automatically deleted. Audit them before using
historical totals in BI. CDR v2 routing_duration maps to Ring Duration,
handling_duration to Talk Duration (including hold time), and call_duration
to Total Duration. These are call-level metrics, not employee activity time.
Dates retain the existing PBX-local convention. Existing recording filenames
are preserved because the v2 list does not supply them.

After upgrading the module, configure the following in the test database:

1. Assign each employee's Yeastar PBX and extension. Employees without an
   extension are excluded. An employee is eligible when end_date is empty or
   today or later; the legacy database active column is not used. A duplicate
   eligible assignment is reported as an error rather than resolved arbitrarily.
2. Set the default Yeastar activity type in Activity Configuration.
3. In the PBX Webhook tab, set PBX Timezone to the timezone used by Yeastar
   (for example Europe/Madrid), then select Create Activities.
4. The Process Yeastar Webhooks scheduled action runs every minute. It can
   also be run manually from Scheduled Actions. A call must have received no
   new events for at least one minute before it is processed. Enabling this
   mode also processes previously captured events, using current employee
   assignments. Keep Capture Only selected until ready to import that history.

Only answered Inbound and Outbound CDR legs involving a configured employee
are eligible. Internal calls, unanswered calls and virtual queue/IVR legs do
not create activities or recording references. One activity is created per
PBX, call ID and employee; additional external legs, including a return after
a transfer, extend the same activity. A pre-existing manual activity with
that company, employee and call ID is reused. Its duration is reconciled on
first import; subsequent legs add their duration without erasing later manual
adjustments. Ambiguous existing activities are reported as errors.

The activity duration is the CDR talk duration minus observed HOLD intervals,
including the hold during an internal transfer consultation. Overlapping hold
intervals are counted once. Yeastar state notifications have no occurrence
timestamp: hold durations use reception timestamps, clipped to the CDR leg and
rounded to seconds. Delayed or missing notifications can therefore affect this
deduction. Internal consultation CDRs are never added to external call time.
PBX timestamps are converted to UTC using PBX Timezone; ambiguous/nonexistent
daylight-saving timestamps are retained as errors for review.

Processed Calls on the PBX shows the permanent call/employee/activity links and
the external legs, including original CDR durations, excluded hold time, CDR
UIDs, call note IDs and exact recording filenames. These records survive raw
webhook cleanup, preventing duplicate activities and durations on redelivery.
If a linked activity is deleted, new legs are reported as errors rather than
silently recreating it. Phone contacts are matched exactly against their raw
or compact number; ambiguous matches leave the activity's party empty.

An error rolls back the whole call's processing without blocking other calls.
Inspect Processing Message on its events, correct the configuration and use
Retry Processing on the errored events. Pending state/transfer events are kept
until the call is closed; incomplete captures remain available for inspection.
No activity is created for a call that has not supplied an eligible final CDR.

Automatic recordings and Gladia
-------------------------------

Configure the existing Gladia credentials in trytond.conf::

    [gladia]
    url = https://api.gladia.io/v2
    api_key = <your-api-key>

After upgrading the module and restarting Tryton, Process Yeastar Recordings
runs every minute. Automatic Gladia Transcription is enabled by default on a
PBX; combined with Create Activities mode and a configured API key, it enqueues
external recording references from processed calls, including historical ones.
Turn this setting off to stop automatically enqueueing recordings. Already
queued jobs continue processing. A copied PBX has automatic transcription off.

The job downloads the exact recording named in the external CDR and attaches
it to the activity. Internal and unanswered calls are excluded. The original
audio is uploaded without a lossy MP3 conversion, preserving channels. It is
uploaded once, submitted to POST /v2/pre-recorded and subsequently polled with
GET /v2/pre-recorded/<id>. Each cron pass advances one stage; no web request
waits for Gladia to finish. Queued/processing jobs retain the same provider ID.
Gladia HTTP requests and PBX requests have connection/read timeouts.

The model is Solaria-1 with language_config.languages set to ca, es, en and
code_switching enabled. Speaker diarization is automatic, without imposing a
speaker count. Utterance text, timestamps, language, speaker labels and channel
information are retained. A speaker index distinguishes voices in one recording;
it does not identify a named person across different recordings.

The primary language is selected by summing the duration of Gladia's timed
utterances in each language, not by counting words. These are detected speech
segment durations, not employee-accounted minutes. For ties within a recording,
Gladia's ranked language list is used, followed by ca/es/en. The original mixed
transcript is preserved. Two additional transcript rows contain translations
into the other two working languages, with their localized subject and summary.
All three translation targets are requested in the initial job because the
primary language is not known beforehand; the primary-language translation is
not published as a redundant fourth transcript. This requests three translation
targets from Gladia, even though only two are displayed.

Gladia Audio-to-LLM generates a structured subject and summary in all three
languages in that same job. This provides language-controlled summaries without
a second audio transcription request; the fixed-format summarization add-on
has no output-language parameter. Generated JSON is parsed and validated, never
executed. Missing/failed translations or activity texts are shown as errors and
the raw provider result remains available for inspection.

For multiple recordings in one activity, language durations are aggregated
across recordings. Ties follow recording order, then ca/es/en. Each recording's
summary is selected in the activity's primary language and appended in recording
order to Description. Subjects are combined into one concise Subject. The last
generated text is tracked: later segments or reprocessing replace generated
content but preserve manual edits and pre-existing manually written text. The
accounted activity duration is never changed by transcription or recording
length. Existing manual activities retain their authored subject/description;
their generated summary is still available in the conversation tab.

The conversation tab now shows recordings, states, primary language, percentages
and the original/translated transcripts. Per-call speaker/language input is no
longer needed. Legacy settings and LLM results remain in the database. Queue
Call Recordings can enqueue missing recordings manually without starting STT
inline. For old activities without webhook legs it retains the configured
recording-list page lookup, matching only external calls for that employee.

Retry resumes the failed stage, preserving uploaded audio, accepted job IDs
and received results. Temporary download/upload/poll errors use exponential
backoff, with at most five attempts before manual retry. A missing recording
can therefore be retried after the PBX has finished preparing it.

The submitting state is committed before the billable POST. If the HTTP reply
is lost, malformed, or a worker is interrupted, the job becomes Submission
Status Unknown rather than submitting the same audio again. Find the accepted
job in Gladia using custom_metadata.tryton_job and custom_metadata.database,
enter its Gladia Job ID as an administrator, then use Resume with Gladia Job ID.
An interrupted submitting job is marked unknown after ten minutes. Transcribe
Again explicitly starts a new billable job for completed/failed results; old
job IDs/results are retained, and the existing audio attachment is reused.

See Gladia's current documentation:

* https://docs.gladia.io/api-reference/v2/pre-recorded/init
* https://docs.gladia.io/chapters/pre-recorded-stt/transcription-process
* https://docs.gladia.io/chapters/audio-intelligence/audio-to-llm
* https://docs.gladia.io/chapters/language/supported-languages

Retention
---------

Clean Yeastar Webhooks runs daily. Webhook Retention (Months) defaults to 3;
set it to 0 to disable cleanup for that PBX. Only Processed or Discarded events
older than that many calendar months are deleted. If any event of the same
PBX/call is pending, errored or recent, the entire captured call is retained.
Capture Only events are never removed automatically while still pending.

Activities, attachments, transcriptions, and the permanent processed-call/leg
identities and recording jobs are not deleted by this scheduled action. All
scheduled actions respect their company context and are installed once;
their schedules can be adjusted through the usual Tryton interface.


